  "id": "DrugBank_DB09096 - MONDO_11292",
  "verbalization": "Benzoyl Peroxide is implicated in the treatment of Atopic Eczema, a condition that can progress to Dermatitis, Atopic. This relationship is supported by multiple pathways, all of which indicate that Benzoyl Peroxide is used in conjunction with other drugs that target different mechanisms of action in the treatment of Atopic Eczema. The evidence suggests that Benzoyl Peroxide may act as a first-line treatment for Atopic Eczema, with subsequent use of drugs like Crisaborole, Pimecrolimus, and Dupilumab, which are often prescribed for more severe or refractory cases. These drugs target various pathways, including inflammation and immune modulation, which are central to the pathophysiology of Atopic Eczema. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism by which Benzoyl Peroxide may influence the progression or management of Atopic Eczema, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence supports a role for Benzoyl Peroxide in the treatment of Atopic Eczema, with the broader context of its use in combination with other therapeutic agents.",
